T(34, "34-6", "Ada and the Thinking Machine", "Recount (Historical)", [
"In 1815, in London, a baby girl named Augusta Ada Byron was born into one of the most talked-about families in England. Her father was Lord Byron, the celebrated poet, but he left the country when Ada was only a few months old, and she scarcely knew him. She was raised by her mother, Annabella, who was determined that her daughter should have a very different upbringing from her father’s, one grounded in logic, mathematics and science rather than poetry alone.",
"Ada was taught by private tutors, and she proved to have a gift for numbers and an even stronger gift for imagination. As a child, she wrote about designing a machine that could carry a person through the air, studying the anatomy of birds to work out how wings might be built. She never built it, but the attempt shows a mind that treated the impossible as a puzzle to be solved, not a wall to stop at.",
"At seventeen, Ada was introduced to Charles Babbage, a mathematician and inventor who was designing machines to do calculations. His first design, the Difference Engine, was intended to produce accurate tables of numbers, which were otherwise copied out by hand and often filled with errors. Later, he conceived something far bolder, the Analytical Engine, a machine that could be given a set of instructions and follow them, and that, in principle, could be set to solve many kinds of problem, not just one. Ada was fascinated, and the two became friends, writing to each other for many years.",
"In 1842, an Italian engineer, Luigi Menabrea, published an article in French describing the Analytical Engine. Ada was asked to translate it into English. She did much more than translate. She added her own notes, which, when finished, were roughly three times as long as the article itself. In them, she set out, step by step, how the engine could be instructed to calculate a particular list of numbers. Many people today regard this as the first computer program, although the machine was never completed and so the program was never run.",
"Yet Ada’s most striking idea was not about arithmetic at all. She realised that if the engine could handle symbols rather than only numbers, and if the rules of music could be described using them, then the machine might one day compose music of its own. At a time when calculating machines were seen as little more than clever adding devices, this was an extraordinary leap. She also insisted, with care, that the machine could only do what people told it to do and could not originate anything for itself, a warning that people still discuss.",
"Ada’s notes were published in 1843 under her initials, A.A.L., and for many years afterwards they were largely overlooked. It was only in the twentieth century, when real computers were being built, that people rediscovered how much she had foreseen. Today a computer programming language carries her name, and every October people celebrate a day in her honour. She was not a great builder of machines; she was something rarer, a person who saw, before any of the machines existed, what they might become.",
], [
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 6, what does the word “overlooked” mean?",opts=["Carefully checked","Praised and copied","Not noticed or given attention","Hidden on purpose"],a="C"),
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="What was Ada’s father famous for being?",a="A poet (Lord Byron).",g="Accept poet."),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the name of the machine that could be given a set of instructions and follow them.",a="Analytical Engine",g="Accept “the Analytical Engine”."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each sentence.",items=["Ada’s mother wanted her to study mathematics and science.","Ada built her flying machine as a child.","Ada’s notes were longer than the article she translated.","The Analytical Engine was finished before 1843."],a=["T","F","T","F"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the events from 1 to 4 to show the order they happened in.",items=["Ada was asked to translate an article.","Ada’s notes were rediscovered by later people.","Ada was introduced to Charles Babbage.","Ada’s notes were published."],a=[2,4,1,3],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why do you think Annabella wanted Ada to study logic and mathematics?",a="She wanted Ada to be different from her father, who was a poet.",g="Accept any answer linked to a different upbringing from her father’s. Do not accept “because Ada was clever” alone."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="The writer says Ada “treated the impossible as a puzzle to be solved, not a wall to stop at”. What does this suggest about Ada’s character?",a="That she was determined, curious and imaginative and did not give up when something seemed impossible.",g="Accept any one or two of: determined, curious, imaginative, brave, creative."),
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="What warning did Ada give about the engine?",a="That it could only do what people told it to do / it could not originate anything itself.",g="Accept any answer about the machine being unable to think of new things alone."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Why does the writer call Ada’s idea about music “an extraordinary leap”?",a="Machines at the time were seen as little more than adding devices (1), yet Ada imagined one could use symbols to compose music (1).",g="1 mark for the idea that machines were only thought of as calculators. 1 mark for the idea that Ada saw further, such as making music. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these best describes paragraph 4?",opts=["It explains why Ada left London.","It describes Ada’s notes and what they set out to do.","It explains how Babbage built his first machine.","It lists Ada’s hobbies as a child."],a="B"),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=2,q="The writer ends, “She was not a great builder of machines; she was something rarer.” Explain what the writer means.",a="Ada did not build machines (1) but she had the rare vision to foresee what machines could become (1).",g="1 mark for the idea that Ada did not construct machines. 1 mark for the idea of vision or foresight about the future. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why do you think Ada’s notes were largely overlooked for many years?",a="The machine was never built and there were no real computers, so people could not see how important her ideas were.",g="Accept any sensible reason linked to no working machine or no computers at the time."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which sentence best sums up the whole text?",opts=["Ada Byron lived in London and wrote letters to her friends.","Charles Babbage built the first working computer.","Lord Byron was a famous poet who left England.","Ada saw early on what computers could become, and her ideas were valued much later."],a="D"),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the year in which Ada’s notes were published.",a="1843",g="Accept 1843 only."),
])
